issue_context.body:
### 🚀 Feature Request


When writing your own fixture that listens to all requests on the page, calling methods, for example, text() / toJson() causes the creation of a new step in the report. This behavior is noisy and generally seems unnecessary. I would like to be able to disable adding such steps to the report.

### Example

Let's take a simple example and look at the resulting html-report
```typescript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test'

test.describe('Example', () => {
  test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
    page.on('response', async (response) => {
      const textResponse = await response.text()

      // some checks on response
      expect.soft(textResponse).toContain('some text')
    })
  })
  test('Some executed requests', async ({ page }) => {
    await page.evaluate(async () => {
      await fetch('https://swapi.dev/api/planets/1/')
      await fetch('https://swapi.dev/api/planets/1/')
      await fetch('https://swapi.dev/api/planets/1/')
      await fetch('https://swapi.dev/api/planets/1/')
      await fetch('https://swapi.dev/api/planets/1/')
    })
  })
})
```
![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f50bdcb8-1263-44c0-811c-7f5b1cec5713)

All calls to the response.text() method are automatically logged into the report and clutter up the overall understanding of what happened in the test. As the number of requests increases, things get much worse.

The problem is that I can’t hide these calls on my own. Yes I can wrap them in test.step and group them in some way, but that won't solve the noise problem


### Motivation

In longer tests, there can often be hundreds of network requests which fill the UI and make it tricky to find steps related to actual test assertions. If Playwright supported hiding these, it could make test reports much simpler to parse.
